ry minimum quantity of =0Aattention, and just outsourced their voting topro=
xy advisory services =0A<https://bloom.bg/4xVCeYY>; these days that is a bi=
t more controversial so they =0Aoutsource it to AI <https://bloom.bg/4ADW51=
z> instead.)=0A=0AOther shareholders don=E2=80=99t pay attention, and don=
=E2=80=99t vote. In particular, retail =0Ashareholders stereotypically don=
=E2=80=99t vote. This is generally very rational of =0Athem: Voting 10 shar=
es of stock takes time and has essentially no benefit.=0A=0AWhat happens to=
 their votes=3F If 40% of shareholders vote for some proposal and =0A30% vo=
te against and 30% don=E2=80=99t vote, then it gets =E2=80=9Ca majority of =
votes cast=E2=80=9D =0Aand probably passes. Butsome things <https://bloom.b=
g/4rJt14C> (mergers, etc.) =0Arequire =E2=80=9Ca majority of shares outstan=
ding,=E2=80=9D and in that case it wouldn=E2=80=99t pass. =0AThat is, if yo=
u don=E2=80=99t vote your stock, then:=0A=0A * Usually you are letting the =
people who do vote decide for you, except =0A * For some very important cor=
porate decisions, you are effectively voting No. =0AThose are pretty intuit=
ive outcomes! Like:=0A=0A * The people who don=E2=80=99t pay attention real=
ly might rationally prefer to defer =0Ato the people who do pay attention, =
but =0A * Big important fundamental changes should only happen if a majorit=
y of =0Ashareholders pay attention and agree to them. =0AFine. There are ot=
her possibilities, though. We talk occasionally =0A<https://bloom.bg/4jEPLA=
u> about =E2=80=9Cretail shareholders who don=E2=80=99t care should be =0Aa=
llowed to sell their voting rights to people who do care,=E2=80=9D which ha=
s an =0Aobvious appeal but mostlyhasn=E2=80=99t taken off <https://bloom.bg=
/4rI4Bsc>. But =0Aanother possibility is:=0A=0A * If you don=E2=80=99t vote=
, you should let the company=E2=80=99s management decide for you. =0AThat i=
s, in some abstract model of democracy, =E2=80=9Cif you don=E2=80=99t care,=
 you=E2=80=99re =0Aletting the people who do care decide=E2=80=9D makes sen=
se. But if you bought shares in =0Aa company, that might suggest =E2=80=94 =
at least as a default =E2=80=94 that you trust the =0Amanagers of the compa=
ny. If you don=E2=80=99t care about the details of some vote, =0Amaybe =E2=
=80=9Clet management decide=E2=80=9D better captures your unexpressed desir=
es than =0A=E2=80=9Clet the shareholders who do care decide.=E2=80=9D Maybe=
 the shareholders who do care =0Aare not representative of the ones who don=
=E2=80=99t; maybe management is.=0A=0AAnd so, if there=E2=80=99s some share=
holder proposal like =E2=80=9Cthe company should write a =0Areport about cl=
imate change,=E2=80=9D and 40% vote yes and 30% vote no and 30% don=E2=80=
=99t =0Avote, maybe thatreally means that 40% want the company to do someth=
ing =0Adifferent and 60% =E2=80=94 the nos and the nonvoters =E2=80=94 thin=
k =E2=80=9Cmeh it=E2=80=99s fine as it is.=E2=80=9D =0AOr if the company as=
ks shareholders to vote to approve executive pay, and 30% =0Avote yes and 4=
0% vote no and 30% don=E2=80=99t vote, maybe thatreally means that 40% of =
=0Ashareholders are engaged and angry and don=E2=80=99t want the executives=
 to get paid =0Athat much, but the other 60% are like =E2=80=9Cehh it=E2=80=
=99s fine, we like management.=E2=80=9D=0A=0AI=E2=80=99m not sure that=E2=
=80=99s correct: Retail shareholders could be pretty annoyed with =0Amanage=
ment, but still not annoyed enough to go and fill out proxy cards for =0Ath=
eir small holdings. But you could ask them. Bloomberg=E2=80=99sTodd Gillesp=
ie =0Areported Monday <https://bloom.bg/4hASnwC>:=0A=0AGoldman Sachs Group =
Inc. plans to give individual shareholders the option of =0Aletting the boa=
rd of directors decide how their votes will be cast, a year =0Aafter coming=
 closer than ever to having its executive compensation proposal =0Arejected=
.=0A=0ARegulators gave a green light to the firm=E2=80=99s request to give=
 retail investors =0Athe option of voting with the board by default, accord=
ing to a Securities and =0AExchange Commission letter Monday. Those investo=
rs account for roughly 30% of =0AGoldman=E2=80=99s shares, according to a p=
erson familiar with the matter.=0A=0AThe change is likely to increase turno=
ut in a way the boosts support for the =0Acompany=E2=80=99s position on key=
 proposals at annual general meetings, where in recent =0Ayears Goldman has=
 faced significant objection to large pay packages for its top =0Aexecutive=
s.=0A=0AAnd Tuesday the US Securities and Exchange Commission granted Tesla=
 Inc. =0Apermission <https://bloom.bg/4iT0FT2> to implement a similar progr=
am in which =0Aretail investors could default to letting Elon Musk vote the=
ir shares. I mean, =0Anot literally Elon Musk; theshares would be voted <ht=
tps://bloom.bg/4AHY7xk> =0A=E2=80=9Cbased on the recommendation of the comp=
any=E2=80=99s board of directors.=E2=80=9D=0A=0AThis approach was pioneered=
 by ExxonMobil Corp., and we talked about it a few =0Amonths ago <https://b=
loom.bg/4jzyM2A>. An Exxon shareholder complained that, if =0A=E2=80=9Calwa=
ys vote with the board=E2=80=9D is an option available to shareholders, the=
n =0A=E2=80=9Calways vote against the board,=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Calways vo=
te the way proxy advisers =0Asuggest,=E2=80=9D should also be options. I ju=
st think that=E2=80=99s wrong=3F I understand why =0Athis option annoys goo=
d-governance advocates, because it really does have the =0Aeffect of entren=
ching management and giving the board free rein to run the =0Acompany. But =
also =E2=80=9Cgive the board free rein to run the company=E2=80=9D really i=
s a =0Aplausible model of what inattentive retail shareholders want. Especi=
ally at =0ATesla.=0A=0AIncidentally, we  talked yesterday <https://bloom.bg=
/4rF3Pw9> about the =0Aprospects that agentic artificial intelligence will =
eliminate retail-investor =0Ainattention. (Which will be bad for banks, whi=
ch rely on inattention, and very =0Avery good for Robinhood, which relies o=
n attention.) I suppose you could tell =0Asome sort of related story here, =
like, =E2=80=9Cretail investors will be able to =0Aactively vote all of the=
ir shares on everything by instructing their AI agent =0Aon what to do.=E2=
=80=9D But what will they tell the agent to do=3F =E2=80=9CVote however you=
 =0Athink is best=E2=80=9D=3F I feel like the simplest answer is =E2=80=9Cj=
ust re-elect the =0Adirectors, approve the pay, and don=E2=80=99t vote for =
any weird proposals=E2=80=9D: That is, =0Ajust defer to management. And at =
Tesla, the retail shareholders willobviously =0Atell their agents =E2=80=9C=
just vote for whatever Elon wants.=E2=80=9D=0A=0A=0AKnicks and Rangers=0A=
=0AThere is a lot of finance theory about conglomerates. If you have a widg=
et =0Acompany, and I have a sprocket company, and we merge them into one co=
mpany, =0Athere are advantages and disadvantages. Some of these are manager=
ial (=E2=80=9Cyou can =0Aapply best-in-class management skills to multiple =
businesses=E2=80=9D vs. =E2=80=9Cthe managers =0Aare unfocused and distract=
ed by multiple businesses=E2=80=9D) but some are more =0Acorporate finance-=
y. The main financial advantages and disadvantages of =0Aconglomerates are:=
=0A=0A * A conglomerate that combines two uncorrelated businesses is safer =
than =0Aeither business alone: Sometimes widget sales are down but sprocket=
 sales are =0Aup, meaning that the company can muddle through a widget down=
turn without going =0Abankrupt. The conglomerate is more attractive to debt=
 investors because its =0Arisks offset each other, and debt investors hate =
risk. =0A * But =E2=80=9Ca basket of options <https://bloom.bg/4rEk6RO> is =
worth more than an =0Aoption on a basket=E2=80=9D: Shareholders might prefe=
r to own a widget company and a =0Asprocket company separately, because the=
y get the upside on each investment but =0Atheir downside is floored at zer=
o. If the widget industry dies out but the =0Asprocket industry booms, a sh=
areholder of separate companies does well: Her =0Awidget investment goes to=
 zero, her sprocket investment returns 1,000%, and =0Ashe=E2=80=99s happy. =
But if she owns a widget/sprocket conglomerate, it might spend =0Ayears los=
ing money on widgets, subsidized by its sprocket profits; its widget =0Alos=
ses arenot floored at zero. The conglomerate might be less attractive to =
=0Aequity investors, because its risks offset each other, and equity invest=
ors =0Alove risk. =0AThere is another, related but slightly different set o=
f investor-targeting =0Aadvantages and disadvantages:=0A=0A * Some investor=
s might really like widgets, and some might really like =0Asprockets. If yo=
u run a widget/sprocket conglomerate, you might attract both =0Asets of inv=
estors, and so appeal to more investors than either company alone. =0A * Al=
ternatively, if you run a widget/sprocket conglomerate, you might attract =
=0Aneitherset of investors. There might be specialized widget investors who=
 ignore =0Ayou, and specialized sprocket investors who ignore you. If you s=
eparated into =0Atwo focused companies they might each be able to attract n=
ew investors. You can =0Atell a clearer story (rather, two clearer stories)=
 to the market. =0A * And if an investor did want a diversified widgets-and=
-sprockets bet, she =0Acould just buy shares of both companies. You don=E2=
=80=99t have to give her that =0Adiversified bet at the corporate level; sh=
e can do it herself. =0AThis is pretty standard stuff. We have talked about=
 all of it in connection =0Awith <https://bloom.bg/4rEy3PI> Elon Musk=E2=80=
=99s conglomerating and =0Ade-conglomerating impulses.=0A=0ASeparately, we =
talked a few months ago about a sports gambling exchange-traded =0Afund <ht=
tps://bloom.bg/4B1pxyL>. Obviously I loved it, but I was a bit =0Askeptical=
. The sports gambling ETF would manage a diversified portfolio of =0Asport=
s bets. This would make it less risky than a sports gambling ETF that bet =
=0Aonly on, like, =E2=80=9Cthe Knicks will win the NBA championship.=E2=80=
=9D As a product for =0Ayour retirement fund, sure, makes sense, I guess. (=
=3F=3F=3F) But =E2=80=A6 it=E2=80=99s =E2=80=A6 sports =E2=80=A6 =0Agamblin=
g=3F Presumablymuch of the appeal of sports gambling is, like, picking the =
=0Ateam you want or expect to win and betting on them=3F And so a sports ga=
mbling =0AETF that is like =E2=80=9Cwe=E2=80=99ll do some sports gambling f=
or you and give you the =0Aproceeds=E2=80=9D is missing an important part o=
f the appeal of sports gambling. I=E2=80=99ve=0Apointed out <https://bloom.=
bg/4rQY6TV> that really what you want is, like, a =0AKnicks ETF, because th=
en people who like the Knicks could bet on your ETF.=0A=0AThe point here is=
, maybe, that diversification in sports betting is bad, at =0Athe fund/prod=
uct level. Some people want to bet on the Knicks, some people want =0Ato be=
t on the Spurs, some people want to bet on the Clippers, but (1) very few =
=0Apeople want a diversified bet on all of them and (2) if they do, they ca=
n =0Aconstruct it themselves. =E2=80=9CHere=E2=80=99s a diversified sports =
bet=E2=80=9D is just going to =0Aappeal to fewer people than =E2=80=9Chere=
=E2=80=99s a Knicks bet.=E2=80=9D=0A=0AAnyway <https://bloom.bg/3Vo4rtZ>:=
=0A=0AThe New York Knicks and Rangers will soon be available for purchase s=
eparately =0Aon the stock market.=0A=0AMSG Sports, which owns the NBA and N=
HL teams, announced Wednesday =0A<https://bloom.bg/47tV2Ul> that it will sp=
lit the two franchises into separate =0Apublicly traded companies after its=
 board of directors approved the move this =0Aweek. The company has explore=
d the move since earlier this year and expects to =0Acomplete the transacti=
on Oct. 26. The spin-off comes after a year of =0Ahigh-profile success for =
the Knicks, who won the NBA title in June, and as the =0ARangers work throu=
gh a rebuild after missing the playoffs the last two seasons.=0A=0AMSG Spor=
ts, currently a publicly traded company, will become MSG =0AKnickerbockers =
Corp. It will own the Knicks and Westchester Knicks (their G =0ALeague team=
). MSG Rangers Corp. will be comprised of the Rangers, the MSG =0Atraining =
facility and the Hartford Wolf Pack (the Rangers=E2=80=99 AHL team).=0A=0AI=
 mean, sure. Presumably there is a market for, like, =E2=80=9Chere=E2=80=99=
